Well, the old rogue7 source had food consumption double when the rogue
was >60% burdened, and triple when >80% burdened.  This added keeping
one's pack clear of trash to the management task of the player, and
nicely affected game balance.  On the whole it was a lot of fun.

In contrast, Keef the Thief can carry everything he finds, no weight or
pack slot limits, and that works for that game pretty well, since there
are some things (a Clydesdale Horse, e.g.) you have to cart around
forever (and can't even ride!  ;-), until you encounter the one special
situation in the game where they are crucial.

Nethack probably falls closer to the first case, but there are certainly
times in the game when I have lots of "stuff", don't know how much will
be of use, am not ready to drop any yet, want to pick up "just one more
goody", and can't 'cause I'm overburdened.  Should I also pay a food
penalty for my lack of knowledge of what _may_ be useful?  I don't know
what that would do to game balance, but I suspect #define-ing HARD will
seem like child's play in comparision.

